*.gz
*.tar.gz
metadata/items_alltext.Rdata
metadata/**/*.csv
metadata/**/*.txt

# ...except the pipeline's own outputs. These are derived files, but git is
# where the weekly diff lives: .github/workflows/metadata-pipeline.yml
# regenerates them on a runner, and `git diff` against the committed copy IS
# the review surface that used to be a mailed log. Committing them also puts
# them back on Ben's disk (a pull) for the manual upload_meta.py step, and
# gives the metadata a history, the way table_audit_report.md already has one.
#
# Listed one by one on purpose. A blanket un-ignore would also sweep in the
# *.diff.csv files, which every run rewrites, plus the provenance, cache and
# scratch CSVs beside them -- and every pull request would then be noise.
!metadata/metadata.csv
!metadata/biblio.csv
!metadata/comps_biblio.csv
!metadata/nominal_biblio.csv
!metadata/simsyn_biblio.csv
!metadata/tags.csv
!metadata/nominal_tags.csv
!metadata/comps_metadata.csv
!metadata/nominal_metadata.csv
!metadata/conj_metadata.csv
!metadata/conj_outcomes.csv
!metadata/conj_biblio.csv
##conj has no dictionary sheet: its union provenance and pending rows are the only
##record of which biblio rows were machine-written or held back, as for core below.
!metadata/conj_biblio_provenance.csv
!metadata/conj_biblio_pending.csv
##rankings (18_rankings.R, 2026-10-15): same shape as conj, same reason.
!metadata/rank_metadata.csv
!metadata/rank_biblio.csv
!metadata/rank_biblio_provenance.csv
!metadata/rank_biblio_pending.csv
!metadata/simsyn_metadata.csv
!metadata/itemtext_metadata.csv
!metadata/collections.csv
!metadata/collection_members.csv
# Curated citation inputs, reviewed against their recorded primary sources.
!metadata/bibtex_overrides.csv
# Public per-table change record (#2168), hand-appended; irw_site renders it.
!metadata/table_changes.csv
# Source caveats shown on landing pages and by the MCP (#2529), hand-appended.
!metadata/data_notes.csv
# Table -> processing-script map for get_processing_notes (#2494); stage 13.
!metadata/table_scripts.csv
# Hand-kept rows stage 13 merges in, for names built at run time (#2529).
!metadata/table_scripts_manual.csv
# Per-column codebook rows the table pages render (#2763); stage 14.
!metadata/column_docs.csv
# Per-table coverage matrix over the live corpus (stage 17, the monthly data-QC
# audit); its coverage_report.md beside it is not ignored by the rules above.
!metadata/coverage_matrix.csv
# Source codebook files found by name in each deposit, and what was checked
# (#2766); find_codebook_links.py, run by hand. Its listing cache stays local.
!metadata/codebook_links.csv
!metadata/codebook_links_checked.csv
!metadata/codebook_by_review.csv
metadata/logs/codebook_listings.json
metadata/logs/codebook_readme_text.json
metadata/logs/codebook_table_names.json
# Aggregator registry (openESM, ...) keyed by the dictionary's `Source via` (#2421).
!metadata/aggregators.csv
# Covariate value labels (#1775): the irw_meta table and the generator's
# hand-reviewed institution list and coverage record. Manual, not weekly.
!metadata/covariate_labels.csv
!metadata/covariate_labels/institutions.csv
!metadata/covariate_labels/coverage.csv
##gaps.csv is the ratchet's BASELINE, so it has to be committed: CI
##reads it to tell a known gap from a new one, and an ignored file
##would make all 674 read as new on every run.
!metadata/covariate_labels/gaps.csv

##The dictionary union's audit trail (#1732). Tracked, unlike 03_tags.R's
##tags_provenance.csv, which this file excludes and the weekly runner therefore
##regenerates and throws away -- leaving no record of which cells a machine
##wrote. It changes only when an automated row lands, so it is not the per-run
##noise the note above is guarding against.
!metadata/biblio_provenance.csv
##Rows held back because their table is not published yet (#1732). Committed for
##the same reason: it is the only place "waiting on a publish" is written down.
!metadata/biblio_pending.csv
##01_metadata.R's rolling-refresh rotation: which existing tables had their
##stats re-fetched, and when. Committed because the weekly run is on Actions,
##where an ignored file starts empty every time -- the rotation then fell back
##to its alphabetical tiebreak and re-checked the same first 200 tables every
##week from 09-04 to 10-02. One row per table, sorted by name, so the weekly
##diff is the 200 timestamps that moved.
!metadata/metadata_refresh_log.csv
##16_conjoint.R's carry-forward stamps (#2970): per live conjoint table, the
##Redivis updatedAt/numRows/hash the row was computed under, its attr_ and
##outcome columns, and the design-check observations. Committed for the same
##reason as the refresh log above: without it the Actions runner recomputes all
##973 tables every week (33 minutes). One row per table, sorted by name.
!metadata/conj_refresh_stamps.csv
##The same for the rankings family (18_rankings.R, #3039).
!metadata/rank_refresh_stamps.csv
##Tier-0 per-item statistics (19_item_classical.py, #1710) are NOT committed,
##unlike every other pipeline output above, and the reason is size: one row per
##table-item is 1,125,820 rows and 78 MB at full corpus coverage, rewritten on
##every run. GitHub warns past 50 MB and refuses past 100, which the next few
##hundred tables would reach. What the repo keeps instead is the four summary
##columns the file is reduced to, which live in metadata.csv and upload with it.
##
##Nothing is lost by regenerating: the whole corpus recomputes in ~16 minutes,
##so the store is a derived artifact like the *.diff.csv files, not state. The
##stamps beside it are per-run bookkeeping for the same reason -- committed,
##they would be 6,651 timestamps changing on every run and nothing else.
##
##Where it IS published is Redivis: `irw_meta.item_classical` (Ben, 2026-10-21 --
##100% coverage on the first run was the trigger he set for that move on 10-18).
##So the file is regenerated before an upload, not read out of git:
##    python3 metadata/19_item_classical.py && cd metadata && python3 \
##      ../.claude/skills/irw-site-update/scripts/upload_meta.py metadata item_classical
metadata/item_classical.csv
metadata/item_classical_stamps.csv
##Which published biblio cells the dictionary corrected on this run (#2001).
##Committed because it is the first run's review surface -- 336 cells across 223
##tables is a list a person can read, where the same change in biblio.csv is a
##223-row diff. It is not per-run noise: the Redivis biblio table is the
##baseline every week, so this file only moves when the sheet does.
!metadata/logs/biblio_refresh_log.csv
!metadata/logs/comps_biblio_refresh_log.csv
!metadata/logs/nominal_biblio_refresh_log.csv
!metadata/logs/conj_biblio_refresh_log.csv
!metadata/logs/simsyn_biblio_refresh_log.csv
##Which citations the run refetched because they named a DOI their row does not
##claim (#2301). Committed for the reason above, one step further along: the
##repair itself is invisible in biblio.csv once the Redivis baseline catches up,
##so this file is where "58 rows were citing someone else's paper and are not
##any more" is written down. Like the refresh log, it moves only when something
##actually drifted -- an ordinary week writes an empty file.
!metadata/logs/biblio_bibtex_refetch_log.csv
!metadata/logs/comps_biblio_bibtex_refetch_log.csv
!metadata/logs/nominal_biblio_bibtex_refetch_log.csv
!metadata/logs/conj_biblio_bibtex_refetch_log.csv
!metadata/logs/simsyn_biblio_bibtex_refetch_log.csv
##Which `Derived_License` cells were derived from `Original License`, and on
##what basis (#2040). Committed for the same reason as the refresh log: the
##review question is "does every one of these read correctly?", which is a short
##list, not a diff. `basis` separates the mechanical carry-forwards from the one
##case that assumes a licence version the source did not state.
!metadata/logs/biblio_license_log.csv
!metadata/logs/comps_biblio_license_log.csv
!metadata/logs/nominal_biblio_license_log.csv
!metadata/logs/conj_biblio_license_log.csv
!metadata/logs/simsyn_biblio_license_log.csv
##Biblio rows dropped because their table is in neither the dictionary nor
##the source's metadata.csv (#2767). Committed so the weekly pipeline PR shows
##every drop for review before it publishes.
!metadata/logs/comps_biblio_drop_log.csv
!metadata/logs/nominal_biblio_drop_log.csv
!metadata/logs/conj_biblio_drop_log.csv
!metadata/logs/simsyn_biblio_drop_log.csv
.env
.Rproj.user
.DS_Store
.Rhistory
itemtext/.cache/
itemtext/skill_test/
itemtext/audit_staging/


# LibreOffice lock files
.~lock.*#

# transient control flag for the itemtext batch loop (its presence is the state)
itemtext/extraction_batches/circuit_breaker.flag
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# red_up packaging artefacts (pip install -e .)
*.egg-info/
irw_validate/results/.shard_index.json

# irw_validate build artefacts. Its package directory IS its project
# directory, so `python -m build` drops these inside the package itself --
# where, uningored, they land in the next diff as a second copy of every
# module.
irw_validate/build/
irw_validate/dist/

##Per-agent fetch caches for concurrent tagging runs (#1704, P5). Same reason
##tags/.claude/skills/irw-auto-tag/.cache is ignored: this is fetched source
##text, much of it copyrighted, and it is a scratch artifact of one run.
tags/.caches/

# Daily version-manifest cron logs. The weekly pipeline logs beside them are
# committed deliberately; these are one line on a normal day and would add ~365
# files a year. Failures alert via a GitHub issue, which carries the log body.
metadata/pipeline_logs/version_manifest_*.log

# Hourly round logs from itemtext/extraction_batches/round_cron.sh. Ignored for the
# same reason as the daily version-manifest logs above: too noisy to track. The
# weekly pipeline's markdown logs ARE tracked, because there is one a week.
itemtext/extraction_batches/cron_logs/

# Raw source downloads fetched by data/ scripts; never committed.
data/*_raw/

# Transient upload staging for the item-text pipeline. red_up reads from here and
# the files are rewritten every batch, so there is nothing to keep -- but on
# 2026-09-08 another session's broad `git add` swept all 48 staged files into an
# unrelated commit (they were already tracked once before, in f107878). Ignoring
# the directory is what stops that recurring; the batch directories under
# itemtext/itemtables/batch_* remain the tracked record of what was extracted.
itemtext/itemtables/clean/

# Local tool state that no one should commit.
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.Rcheck/
